INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antiepileptics','easy','mcq','Which of the following best describes the primary mechanism of action of sodium valproate?','["A. Selective blockade of T-type calcium channels only","B. Blockade of sodium channels only","C. Enhancement of GABA activity and blockade of voltage-gated sodium and T-type calcium channels","D. Inhibition of glutamate release at NMDA receptors"]','A','Sodium valproate has multiple mechanisms: it enhances GABA neurotransmission by inhibiting GABA transaminase, and also blocks voltage-gated sodium and T-type calcium channels. This broad mechanism makes it effective across many seizure types.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antiepileptics','medium','mcq','A 25-year-old woman with epilepsy on phenytoin develops coarse facial features, gingival hyperplasia, and hirsutism. Which mechanism best explains these cosmetic adverse effects?','["A. Enhanced GABA-A receptor activity causing receptor downregulation","B. Inhibition of calcium-dependent neurotransmitter release","C. Long-term blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels causing tissue accumulation","D. Chronic phenytoin use leading to collagen proliferation and hormonal effects via unclear mechanisms"]','B','Phenytoin''s cosmetic side effects -- gingival hyperplasia, hirsutism, and coarse facies -- are well-documented long-term adverse effects. The exact mechanisms involve collagen proliferation stimulated by phenytoin and possible hormonal interactions. These are among the most important adverse effects to monitor in long-term phenytoin therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antiepileptics','medium','mcq','Carbamazepine is first-line for which type of epilepsy?','["A. Absence seizures","B. Myoclonic seizures","C. Focal (partial) seizures","D. Infantile spasms"]','C','Carbamazepine is a first-line agent for focal (partial) seizures, including those with or without secondary generalisation. It works by blocking voltage-gated sodium channels in a use-dependent manner, stabilising hyperexcitable membranes. It is contraindicated in absence and myoclonic seizures, as it can worsen them.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antiepileptics','hard','mcq','A 30-year-old epileptic patient on carbamazepine presents with confusion, hyponatraemia (Na+ 122 mmol/L), and euvolaemia. Which mechanism best explains this electrolyte disturbance?','["A. Carbamazepine depletes sodium via enhanced gastrointestinal loss","B. Carbamazepine blocks aldosterone receptors in the distal nephron","C. Carbamazepine inhibits renal tubular sodium reabsorption directly","D. Carbamazepine potentiates antidiuretic hormone (ADH) action on renal collecting ducts, causing water retention"]','D','Carbamazepine causes syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH) by potentiating the action of ADH on renal collecting ducts, leading to water retention and dilutional hyponatraemia. This is a clinically important adverse effect requiring regular electrolyte monitoring.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antiepileptics','easy','mcq','Levetiracetam''s mechanism of action is unique among antiepileptics. Which best describes it?','["A. Blockade of T-type calcium channels","B. Blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels","C. Binding to synaptic vesicle protein SV2A, modulating neurotransmitter release","D. Enhancement of GABA at GABA-A receptors"]','A','Levetiracetam binds to synaptic vesicle glycoprotein SV2A, a protein involved in vesicle exocytosis. This unique mechanism modulates synaptic neurotransmitter release and contributes to its broad-spectrum antiepileptic efficacy. Unlike most other antiepileptics, it does not affect sodium channels or GABA directly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antiepileptics','medium','mcq','Lamotrigine is used in pregnancy-associated epilepsy. Which drug interaction is most clinically significant when adding lamotrigine to an existing regimen?','["A. Phenytoin increases lamotrigine levels by inhibiting its glucuronidation","B. Sodium valproate markedly increases lamotrigine levels by inhibiting its glucuronidation, risking toxicity","C. Levetiracetam decreases lamotrigine clearance via CYP3A4 inhibition","D. Carbamazepine increases lamotrigine levels via enzyme inhibition"]','B','Sodium valproate inhibits the glucuronidation (UGT enzymes) of lamotrigine, significantly increasing lamotrigine plasma levels and risking serious rash including Stevens-Johnson syndrome. When combining these agents, the lamotrigine dose must be halved. This is a high-yield drug interaction in clinical practice.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antiepileptics','medium','mcq','A 14-year-old girl has staring spells lasting 5-10 seconds associated with 3 Hz spike-wave discharges on EEG. Which antiepileptic drug is the drug of choice?','["A. Carbamazepine","B. Phenytoin","C. Ethosuximide","D. Levetiracetam"]','C','Ethosuximide is the drug of choice for absence seizures. It works by blocking T-type calcium channels in thalamic neurons, which are responsible for the 3 Hz spike-wave rhythm characteristic of absence epilepsy. Carbamazepine and phenytoin are ineffective for absence seizures and can worsen them.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antiepileptics','hard','mcq','Phenytoin exhibits zero-order (saturation) kinetics at therapeutic doses. What is the most important clinical implication of this pharmacokinetic property?','["A. Half-life remains constant across all plasma concentrations making dosing predictable","B. Small dose increases cause disproportionately large rises in plasma concentration, increasing toxicity risk","C. Drug accumulation occurs only at supratherapeutic doses making toxicity rare","D. Phenytoin is safely dosed by body weight without monitoring"]','D','At therapeutic plasma concentrations, phenytoin saturates its metabolising enzymes (zero-order kinetics). This means a small increase in dose causes a disproportionately large rise in plasma level, dramatically narrowing the therapeutic window. Close monitoring of plasma levels and incremental dose adjustments are essential to avoid toxicity (nystagmus, ataxia, cerebellar signs).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antiepileptics','easy','mcq','A woman of childbearing age is started on valproate for epilepsy. Which monitoring and counselling points are most critical?','["A. Monitor serum lithium levels; avoid dairy products","B. Monitor full blood count monthly; avoid driving only","C. Counsel regarding teratogenicity (neural tube defects), supplement folic acid 5 mg daily, and use effective contraception; monitor LFTs and FBC","D. Valproate is safe in pregnancy; monitor renal function only"]','A','Valproate is a major teratogen -- it causes neural tube defects (spina bifida), facial abnormalities, and neurodevelopmental delay (fetal valproate syndrome). Folic acid 5 mg/day pre-conception and through the first trimester reduces neural tube defect risk. Women must use effective contraception. LFTs and FBC are monitored for hepatotoxicity and thrombocytopenia respectively.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antiepileptics','medium','mcq','Which antiepileptic drug acts primarily by inhibiting the synaptic reuptake of GABA and is indicated as adjunctive therapy for focal seizures?','["A. Vigabatrin","B. Tiagabine","C. Gabapentin","D. Pregabalin"]','B','Tiagabine inhibits the GABA reuptake transporter (GAT-1), increasing synaptic GABA concentration and prolonging inhibitory neurotransmission. It is used as adjunctive therapy for focal seizures. Vigabatrin irreversibly inhibits GABA transaminase (different mechanism), while gabapentin and pregabalin bind alpha-2-delta calcium channel subunits.','universal',false,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antidepressants','easy','mcq','What is the primary mechanism of action of selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)?','["A. Inhibition of monoamine oxidase A and B","B. Blockade of presynaptic serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake transporters equally","C. Selective inhibition of the serotonin reuptake transporter (SERT), increasing synaptic serotonin","D. Direct agonism at postsynaptic 5-HT1A receptors"]','C','SSRIs selectively block the serotonin reuptake transporter (SERT), preventing reuptake of serotonin into the presynaptic neuron. This increases serotonin availability in the synaptic cleft. SSRIs have minimal effect on noradrenaline or dopamine reuptake, which accounts for their improved tolerability compared to TCAs and MAOIs.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antidepressants','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old woman on fluoxetine for depression is started on tramadol for pain. Three days later she develops agitation, diaphoresis, clonus, and hyperthermia. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Anticholinergic toxidrome","B. Malignant hyperthermia","C. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","D. Serotonin syndrome"]','D','Serotonin syndrome results from excessive serotonergic activity, classically presenting with the triad of cognitive changes (agitation, confusion), autonomic instability (hyperthermia, diaphoresis, tachycardia), and neuromuscular abnormalities (clonus, hyperreflexia, tremor). Tramadol inhibits serotonin reuptake; combined with fluoxetine (SSRI), dangerous serotonergic excess occurs.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antidepressants','medium','mcq','Which class of antidepressants inhibits both serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake and is also licensed for generalised anxiety disorder and neuropathic pain?','["A. SNRIs","B. MAOIs","C. SSRIs","D. TCAs"]','A','Serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs) such as venlafaxine and duloxetine inhibit both SERT and the noradrenaline transporter (NET). Their dual mechanism contributes to efficacy in generalised anxiety disorder (GAD), neuropathic pain, fibromyalgia, and stress urinary incontinence (duloxetine), expanding their clinical utility beyond depression.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antidepressants','hard','mcq','A patient on phenelzine (an MAOI) eats a meal high in tyramine. Which of the following best explains the resulting hypertensive crisis?','["A. Tyramine directly activates alpha-2 adrenoceptors causing vasoconstriction","B. Tyramine, normally metabolised by gut/liver MAO, accumulates and displaces noradrenaline from presynaptic vesicles causing massive sympathetic activation","C. Tyramine inhibits reuptake of noradrenaline by blocking NET in peripheral vessels","D. Tyramine is converted to dopamine by MAO in the adrenal medulla"]','B','MAOIs inhibit monoamine oxidase A in the gut and liver, which normally degrades dietary tyramine. With MAO inhibited, tyramine enters the systemic circulation intact. As an indirect sympathomimetic, tyramine enters adrenergic nerve terminals and displaces stored noradrenaline, causing massive noradrenaline release and life-threatening hypertensive crisis -- the cheese reaction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antidepressants','easy','mcq','Which adverse effect is most characteristic of tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) that limits their use in overdose-prone patients?','["A. Irreversible agranulocytosis","B. Hepatic necrosis at standard doses","C. QTc prolongation and fatal arrhythmias in overdose","D. Irreversible hypertension"]','C','TCAs block cardiac fast sodium channels and potassium channels, causing QRS widening, QT prolongation, and potentially fatal ventricular arrhythmias in overdose. They also cause hypotension via alpha-1 blockade and anticholinergic toxicity. The narrow therapeutic index and cardiovascular lethality in overdose makes them high-risk in suicidal patients, favouring SSRIs as first line.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antidepressants','medium','mcq','A patient on an SSRI reports sexual dysfunction and weight gain after 6 months. Which SSRI is most likely to cause the least weight gain and sexual dysfunction?','["A. Sertraline","B. Citalopram","C. Paroxetine","D. Fluoxetine"]','D','Among SSRIs, fluoxetine is least associated with weight gain and may even cause mild weight loss. Paroxetine has the most significant weight gain and sexual dysfunction profile due to its additional anticholinergic and antihistaminergic properties. Fluoxetine also has the longest half-life (active metabolite norfluoxetine: 1-2 weeks), which makes discontinuation syndrome less likely.','universal',false,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antidepressants','hard','mcq','Which antidepressant mechanism involves antagonism of presynaptic alpha-2 adrenoceptors, leading to increased release of both noradrenaline and serotonin, and is classified as a noradrenergic and specific serotonergic antidepressant (NaSSA)?','["A. Trazodone","B. Venlafaxine","C. Mirtazapine","D. Bupropion"]','A','Mirtazapine blocks presynaptic alpha-2 autoreceptors and heteroreceptors, disinhibiting the release of noradrenaline and serotonin. It also blocks 5-HT2 and 5-HT3 receptors (reducing sexual dysfunction and nausea) and histamine H1 receptors (causing sedation and weight gain). Its sedating profile makes it useful in depressed patients with insomnia or poor appetite.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antidepressants','medium','mcq','A 28-year-old with major depressive disorder asks when she should expect to see an improvement in mood after starting an antidepressant. What is the most accurate response?','["A. Within 24-48 hours of the first dose","B. Typically 2-4 weeks after starting treatment, with full effect at 6-8 weeks","C. Immediately after the first week if the dose is therapeutic","D. Only after 3-6 months of continuous therapy"]','B','Antidepressants typically require 2-4 weeks before significant mood improvement occurs, with maximum benefit seen at 6-8 weeks. This delay is thought to reflect adaptive changes in receptor density and downstream neuroplasticity, not just immediate neurotransmitter changes. Patients must be counselled about this delay to prevent early discontinuation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antidepressants','medium','mcq','Which antidepressant is a dopamine and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor (DNRI) and is also used for smoking cessation?','["A. Trazodone","B. Agomelatine","C. Bupropion","D. Mirtazapine"]','C','Bupropion inhibits reuptake of dopamine and noradrenaline (DNRI). It has no significant serotonergic activity, making it free from sexual dysfunction and serotonin syndrome risk. Its dopaminergic action in the mesolimbic system underlies its use in nicotine dependence (marketed as Zyban). It lowers seizure threshold and is contraindicated in patients with eating disorders or seizure history.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antidepressants','easy','mcq','A patient taking an irreversible MAOI needs to switch to an SSRI. What is the minimum washout period required before starting the SSRI?','["A. 3 days","B. 14 days","C. 28 days","D. 24 hours"]','D','A minimum 14-day washout period is required after stopping an irreversible MAOI before initiating an SSRI. This allows regeneration of monoamine oxidase enzyme activity. Combining MAOIs with SSRIs risks potentially fatal serotonin syndrome. Note: when switching from fluoxetine (long half-life) TO an MAOI, a 5-week washout is required.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antipsychotics','easy','mcq','The antipsychotic efficacy of typical antipsychotics is primarily attributed to which receptor mechanism?','["A. Blockade of muscarinic M1 receptors in the striatum","B. Blockade of serotonin 5-HT2A receptors","C. Blockade of dopamine D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway","D. Agonism at dopamine D1 receptors in the prefrontal cortex"]','A','Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics exert their antipsychotic effect primarily through blockade of dopamine D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway, reducing the hyperactive dopaminergic signalling thought to underlie positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions). D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway causes extrapyramidal side effects, and in the tuberoinfundibular pathway causes hyperprolactinaemia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antipsychotics','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old male with schizophrenia on haloperidol develops sustained involuntary repetitive movements of the tongue and lips after 3 years of treatment. What is this adverse effect and what is the most appropriate management?','["A. Acute dystonia -- treat with intramuscular procyclidine","B. Tardive dyskinesia -- reduce or discontinue antipsychotic if possible; consider switching to clozapine or valbenazine","C. Akathisia -- treat with propranolol or benzodiazepine","D. Pseudoparkinsonism -- treat with oral procyclidine"]','B','Tardive dyskinesia (TD) is a late-onset movement disorder caused by dopamine receptor supersensitivity following chronic D2 blockade. It presents with orofacial dyskinesias (tongue writhing, lip smacking). Management involves reducing or stopping the causative drug, switching to an atypical antipsychotic (particularly clozapine), or using VMAT2 inhibitors (valbenazine, deutetrabenazine).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antipsychotics','medium','mcq','Which atypical antipsychotic requires mandatory white cell count monitoring due to the risk of life-threatening agranulocytosis?','["A. Risperidone","B. Olanzapine","C. Clozapine","D. Quetiapine"]','C','Clozapine causes agranulocytosis in approximately 1-2% of patients, which can be fatal if undetected. Mandatory haematological monitoring is required: weekly FBC for 18 weeks, fortnightly for 12 months, then monthly thereafter. This restricts clozapine to treatment-resistant schizophrenia (failed two adequate antipsychotic trials). Despite this risk, it remains the most effective antipsychotic.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antipsychotics','hard','mcq','A 55-year-old man on haloperidol develops fever (40 degrees C), severe muscle rigidity, autonomic instability, and elevated CK (15,000 U/L). What is the most likely diagnosis and initial treatment?','["A. Malignant hyperthermia -- treat with intravenous dantrolene only","B. Acute dystonic reaction -- treat with procyclidine","C. Serotonin syndrome -- treat with cyproheptadine","D. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) -- stop antipsychotic immediately, provide supportive care, consider dantrolene and bromocriptine"]','D','Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) is a rare but life-threatening reaction to antipsychotics characterised by the tetrad of hyperthermia, severe muscle rigidity (lead pipe), autonomic instability, and altered consciousness, with markedly elevated CK. Treatment requires immediate cessation of the antipsychotic, aggressive supportive care, and pharmacological treatment with dantrolene (muscle relaxant) and/or bromocriptine (dopamine agonist).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antipsychotics','easy','mcq','Which metabolic side effect is most commonly associated with atypical antipsychotics such as olanzapine and clozapine?','["A. Hyperprolactinaemia and galactorrhoea","B. Hypoglycaemia and weight loss","C. Weight gain, dyslipidaemia, and new-onset type 2 diabetes mellitus","D. Hypertension and peripheral oedema"]','A','Atypical antipsychotics, particularly olanzapine and clozapine, cause significant metabolic syndrome: substantial weight gain (via H1 and 5-HT2C antagonism), dyslipidaemia, and new-onset type 2 diabetes. Regular monitoring of weight, fasting glucose, and lipid profiles is mandatory. Hyperprolactinaemia is more characteristic of risperidone and typical antipsychotics.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antipsychotics','medium','mcq','Why do atypical antipsychotics cause fewer extrapyramidal side effects than typical antipsychotics?','["A. Atypicals block 5-HT2A receptors in addition to D2 receptors, allowing dopamine release in the striatum and reducing EPS","B. Atypicals selectively block D4 receptors only, sparing the nigrostriatal pathway completely","C. Atypicals are centrally acting anticholinergics that directly prevent EPS","D. Atypicals have a higher affinity for D2 receptors than typical antipsychotics"]','C','Atypical antipsychotics combine D2 blockade with 5-HT2A blockade. Serotonin normally inhibits dopamine release in the nigrostriatal pathway; by blocking 5-HT2A receptors, atypicals enhance dopamine release in the striatum, offsetting D2 blockade and reducing extrapyramidal side effects. Some atypicals also have rapid D2 dissociation kinetics, further reducing EPS.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antipsychotics','medium','mcq','Which extrapyramidal side effect of antipsychotics presents earliest (within hours to days of initiation) and responds best to anticholinergic medication?','["A. Drug-induced parkinsonism","B. Tardive dyskinesia","C. Akathisia","D. Acute dystonia"]','B','Acute dystonia typically occurs within hours to a few days of starting or increasing a typical antipsychotic. It presents as painful, sustained muscle contractions (e.g., oculogyric crisis, torticollis, tongue protrusion). Treatment with intramuscular or intravenous anticholinergics (procyclidine, benztropine) provides rapid relief. It is more common in young males and with high-potency antipsychotics.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antipsychotics','hard','mcq','Which antipsychotic is a partial agonist at D2 receptors rather than a full antagonist, and how does this affect its profile?','["A. Risperidone -- partial D2 agonism reduces risk of agranulocytosis","B. Amisulpride -- partial D2 agonism eliminates all side effects","C. Quetiapine -- partial agonism causes more EPS than haloperidol","D. Aripiprazole -- partial D2 agonism provides antipsychotic effect while reducing risk of hyperprolactinaemia and metabolic syndrome compared to full D2 antagonists"]','D','Aripiprazole is a partial agonist at D2 and D3 receptors and a partial agonist at 5-HT1A receptors. In hyperdopaminergic states (mesolimbic system), it acts as a functional antagonist, reducing positive symptoms. In the nigrostriatal and tuberoinfundibular pathways where dopamine tone is lower, it acts more as an agonist, reducing EPS and hyperprolactinaemia compared to full D2 antagonists.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antipsychotics','medium','mcq','Which antipsychotic is most appropriate for managing psychosis in a patient with Parkinson''s disease to avoid worsening motor symptoms?','["A. Quetiapine or clozapine at low doses","B. Chlorpromazine","C. Haloperidol","D. Risperidone"]','A','Typical antipsychotics and most atypicals worsen Parkinson''s disease by blocking dopamine receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway. Quetiapine and clozapine have the lowest D2 receptor affinity and are the preferred options. Clozapine is most evidence-based for Parkinson''s psychosis but requires mandatory blood monitoring. Quetiapine is used off-label with a favourable tolerability profile.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Antipsychotics','easy','mcq','A patient on a depot antipsychotic (flupentixol decanoate) develops hyperprolactinaemia. Which symptom would you NOT expect from this condition?','["A. Gynaecomastia in males","B. Hypertension and weight loss","C. Galactorrhoea","D. Amenorrhoea"]','B','Hyperprolactinaemia from D2 blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway causes: galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea/oligomenorrhoea in women; gynaecomastia, galactorrhoea, and erectile dysfunction in men; and reduced bone density (osteoporosis) with long-term exposure. Hypertension and weight loss are not consequences of hyperprolactinaemia -- weight gain is more typical of antipsychotics overall.','universal',false,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Anxiolytics & Sedative-Hypnotics','easy','mcq','What is the primary mechanism of action of benzodiazepines?','["A. Positive allosteric modulation of GABA-A receptors, increasing the frequency of chloride channel opening","B. Blockade of GABA-B receptors reducing inhibitory tone","C. Inhibition of GABA transaminase, increasing synaptic GABA","D. Direct activation of GABA-A chloride channels"]','C','Benzodiazepines bind to a specific allosteric site on the GABA-A receptor (at the interface of alpha and gamma subunits), potentiating the effect of GABA. They increase the frequency of chloride channel opening (without GABA they have no effect). This distinguishes them from barbiturates, which increase the duration of chloride channel opening and can activate the channel directly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Anxiolytics & Sedative-Hypnotics','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old woman prescribed temazepam for insomnia reports needing higher doses after 4 weeks to achieve the same effect. Which pharmacological phenomenon explains this?','["A. Drug allergy causing receptor desensitisation","B. First-pass metabolism increasing causing lower bioavailability over time","C. Pharmacokinetic tolerance due to CYP3A4 induction reducing plasma levels","D. Pharmacodynamic tolerance due to GABA-A receptor downregulation and reduced receptor sensitivity"]','D','Benzodiazepine tolerance develops primarily through pharmacodynamic mechanisms: chronic receptor activation leads to GABA-A receptor downregulation (reduced receptor number), uncoupling of the benzodiazepine binding site, and reduced chloride channel responsiveness. This explains diminishing efficacy over weeks, and underpins withdrawal syndromes when stopped abruptly.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Anxiolytics & Sedative-Hypnotics','medium','mcq','A patient presents with benzodiazepine overdose: drowsy, respiratory rate 8/min, GCS 10, haemodynamically stable. What is the pharmacological reversal agent?','["A. Flumazenil","B. N-acetylcysteine","C. Naloxone","D. Neostigmine"]','A','Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine binding site on GABA-A receptors, rapidly reversing benzodiazepine-induced sedation and respiratory depression. It has a short half-life (about 1 hour), shorter than most benzodiazepines, so re-sedation may occur and repeated doses or infusion may be needed. It should not be used in benzodiazepine-dependent patients due to risk of precipitating seizures.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Anxiolytics & Sedative-Hypnotics','easy','mcq','Buspirone is used as an anxiolytic. How does its mechanism differ from benzodiazepines?','["A. Buspirone enhances GABA-A activity like benzodiazepines but with slower onset","B. Buspirone is a partial agonist at 5-HT1A receptors with no effect on GABA; it has no sedation, dependence, or immediate anxiolytic action","C. Buspirone blocks serotonin reuptake and provides immediate anxiolytic relief","D. Buspirone is a full agonist at GABA-B receptors causing muscle relaxation"]','B','Buspirone is a partial agonist at presynaptic 5-HT1A receptors, reducing serotonin firing. Unlike benzodiazepines, it has no GABAergic activity, no sedation, no anticonvulsant effect, no muscle relaxant effect, no dependence, and no cross-tolerance with alcohol. Its anxiolytic effect takes 2-4 weeks to develop, making it unsuitable for acute anxiety but appropriate for generalised anxiety disorder (GAD).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Anxiolytics & Sedative-Hypnotics','medium','mcq','Z-drugs (zopiclone, zolpidem, zaleplon) differ from classical benzodiazepines in their receptor selectivity. Which of the following best describes this difference?','["A. Z-drugs are selective for GABA-A receptors containing the alpha-1 subunit (sedation), whereas benzodiazepines bind non-selectively to alpha-1, alpha-2, and alpha-3 subunits","B. Z-drugs are full agonists at the benzodiazepine site; benzodiazepines are partial agonists","C. Z-drugs block serotonin to induce sleep while benzodiazepines enhance GABA","D. Z-drugs act on GABA-B receptors whereas benzodiazepines act on GABA-A receptors"]','C','Z-drugs preferentially bind to GABA-A receptors containing the alpha-1 subunit, which mediates sedation and hypnosis. Benzodiazepines are non-selective and also bind alpha-2 (anxiolysis, muscle relaxation) and alpha-3 subunits. This relative selectivity of Z-drugs for sleep-mediating subunits was hoped to reduce dependence and anxiolytic/muscle-relaxant effects, though in practice dependence risk remains significant.','universal',false,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Anxiolytics & Sedative-Hypnotics','hard','mcq','A patient with chronic benzodiazepine dependence is abruptly stopped. Which withdrawal symptom is most life-threatening?','["A. Muscle cramps and diarrhoea","B. Seizures and delirium","C. Depression and lethargy","D. Insomnia and anxiety"]','D','Abrupt cessation of benzodiazepines in dependent patients can cause a severe withdrawal syndrome including anxiety, tremor, sweating, insomnia, and -- most dangerously -- generalised tonic-clonic seizures and delirium. This occurs because of rebound CNS hyperexcitability after chronic GABA-A receptor downregulation. Gradual tapering using a long-acting benzodiazepine (e.g., diazepam) is essential to prevent life-threatening withdrawal.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Anxiolytics & Sedative-Hypnotics','medium','mcq','Which benzodiazepine property determines its suitability for treating acute seizures versus chronic anxiety?','["A. Route of elimination -- renally cleared agents are preferred for seizures","B. Protein binding -- higher protein binding agents are used for acute seizures","C. Half-life and speed of onset -- short-acting IV benzodiazepines (diazepam, lorazepam) for acute seizures; longer-acting agents for anxiety","D. Lipophilicity alone -- lipophilic agents are preferred for all indications"]','A','For acute seizures, benzodiazepines with rapid IV action are required (diazepam, lorazepam, midazolam). Lorazepam is often preferred for status epilepticus due to its longer brain duration despite shorter half-life. For chronic anxiety, longer-acting agents (e.g., diazepam) provide smoother anxiolysis with reduced peaks and troughs. Short-acting agents (alprazolam, lorazepam) carry higher dependence risk.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Anxiolytics & Sedative-Hypnotics','hard','mcq','An anaesthetist uses midazolam for procedural sedation. Which pharmacokinetic property makes it particularly suitable?','["A. Long half-life ensuring prolonged sedation post-procedure","B. Short half-life, high lipophilicity enabling rapid onset and offset, and water solubility at acidic pH for IV preparation","C. High protein binding preventing redistribution to tissues","D. Renal excretion without hepatic metabolism enabling predictable kinetics"]','B','Midazolam is water-soluble at acidic pH (allowing IV formulation without propylene glycol) but becomes lipophilic at physiological pH, enabling rapid CNS penetration. Its short half-life (1.5-2.5 hours) and hepatic oxidative metabolism allow rapid recovery. It also causes anterograde amnesia, desirable for procedural sedation. These properties (rapid onset, short duration, amnesia, reversibility with flumazenil) make it ideal for procedures.','universal',false,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Anxiolytics & Sedative-Hypnotics','medium','mcq','Chlordiazepoxide is commonly used in alcohol withdrawal management. What is the rationale?','["A. It potentiates GABA-A activity, cross-tolerant with alcohol, suppressing CNS hyperexcitability during withdrawal","B. It inhibits alcohol metabolism, prolonging alcohol effect and easing withdrawal","C. It is an NMDA receptor antagonist, blocking glutamate excitotoxicity during withdrawal","D. It blocks dopamine receptors, reducing alcohol cravings in the reward pathway"]','C','Alcohol positively modulates GABA-A receptors and inhibits NMDA receptors. Chronic alcohol use leads to compensatory downregulation of GABA-A and upregulation of NMDA receptors. Abrupt cessation causes dangerous CNS hyperexcitability. Benzodiazepines (chlordiazepoxide, diazepam) are cross-tolerant with alcohol at GABA-A receptors, providing a substitute to taper and preventing withdrawal seizures and delirium tremens.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Anxiolytics & Sedative-Hypnotics','hard','mcq','Which statement correctly distinguishes barbiturates from benzodiazepines in terms of mechanism and safety profile?','["A. Benzodiazepines increase the duration of chloride channel opening and have no ceiling effect","B. Both drugs act identically on GABA-A receptors, differing only in half-life","C. Barbiturates are safer in overdose than benzodiazepines due to a higher therapeutic index","D. Barbiturates increase the duration of chloride channel opening and can activate GABA-A channels directly without GABA, causing dose-dependent respiratory depression with no ceiling effect"]','D','Barbiturates bind to a different site on GABA-A receptors and increase the duration of chloride channel opening. At high doses, barbiturates can directly open the channel without GABA, causing potentially fatal dose-dependent CNS and respiratory depression with no ceiling effect -- hence the very narrow therapeutic index and high lethality in overdose. Benzodiazepines only enhance GABA effect (frequency of opening) and cannot activate the channel alone, giving them a safer overdose profile.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Mood Stabilisers','easy','mcq','Which of the following best describes lithium''s narrow therapeutic index in clinical practice?','["A. Therapeutic range is unpredictable; dose is titrated by symptoms alone","B. Therapeutic range 2.0-3.5 mmol/L; toxicity occurs above 4.0 mmol/L","C. Therapeutic range 0.6-1.0 mmol/L; toxicity becomes clinically significant above 1.5 mmol/L","D. Therapeutic range 5-10 mg/L; monitoring is not required at standard doses"]','A','Lithium''s therapeutic serum level is 0.6-1.0 mmol/L (0.8-1.0 for acute mania). Mild toxicity occurs at 1.5-2.0 mmol/L (tremor, nausea, diarrhoea), moderate at 2.0-2.5 mmol/L (confusion, ataxia, coarse tremor), and severe above 2.5 mmol/L (seizures, coma, cardiac arrhythmias). Levels are checked 12 hours post-dose every 3-6 months once stable.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Mood Stabilisers','medium','mcq','What is the proposed primary mechanism of action of lithium as a mood stabiliser?','["A. Lithium blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, stabilising neuronal firing","B. Lithium inhibits inositol monophosphatase and glycogen synthase kinase-3 (GSK-3), disrupting intracellular signalling pathways","C. Lithium is a GABA-A receptor agonist reducing excitatory neurotransmission","D. Lithium blocks dopamine D2 receptors, acting as an atypical antipsychotic"]','B','Lithium''s mood-stabilising effect is attributed to multiple mechanisms: inhibition of inositol monophosphatase (depleting intracellular inositol, reducing phosphoinositide signalling) and inhibition of GSK-3 (a kinase involved in neuronal plasticity and circadian rhythm regulation). Lithium also modulates serotonin, dopamine, and noradrenaline systems. No single mechanism fully explains its unique prophylactic and antisuicidal effects.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Mood Stabilisers','medium','mcq','A 40-year-old woman on lithium for 8 years presents with polyuria, polydipsia, and dilute urine (osmolality 150 mOsmol/kg). What is the most likely complication?','["A. Lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus due to downregulation of aquaporin-2 in the collecting duct","B. Lithium-induced central diabetes insipidus due to ADH deficiency","C. Lithium-induced hyperparathyroidism causing hypercalcaemia","D. Lithium-induced hyperthyroidism"]','C','Long-term lithium use causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (NDI) in up to 40% of patients by downregulating aquaporin-2 water channels in the renal collecting duct, impairing ADH responsiveness. Unlike central DI, desmopressin is ineffective. Management includes ensuring adequate fluid intake, minimising lithium dose, and using amiloride (which reduces lithium entry into collecting duct cells) if necessary.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Mood Stabilisers','hard','mcq','A patient on lithium is prescribed ibuprofen for arthritis. What interaction should be anticipated and why?','["A. NSAIDs directly displace lithium from plasma proteins, increasing free lithium","B. NSAIDs have no significant interaction with lithium","C. NSAIDs reduce lithium levels by increasing renal lithium clearance","D. NSAIDs increase lithium toxicity by inhibiting prostaglandin-mediated renal lithium excretion, increasing serum lithium levels"]','D','NSAIDs inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, which reduces renal blood flow and afferent arteriole dilation in the kidney. This reduces glomerular filtration rate and decreases renal lithium excretion, causing lithium accumulation and toxicity. ACE inhibitors and diuretics (especially thiazides) have the same effect. This is a high-yield, clinically critical drug interaction requiring lithium level monitoring.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Mood Stabilisers','easy','mcq','Which investigations should be performed before initiating lithium therapy and for ongoing monitoring?','["A. Random blood glucose and HbA1c only","B. Only serum lithium levels every 6 months","C. Baseline and regular thyroid function tests (TFTs), renal function (eGFR, electrolytes), serum lithium levels, and ECG in older patients","D. Liver function tests and FBC only"]','A','Before starting lithium: check renal function (lithium is renally excreted; impaired renal function causes accumulation), thyroid function (lithium causes hypothyroidism in up to 40% by inhibiting thyroid hormone synthesis), and ECG (T-wave changes). Ongoing monitoring: serum lithium levels (12-hour trough), TFTs, and renal function every 3-6 months once stable, with more frequent monitoring during dose changes or intercurrent illness.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Mood Stabilisers','medium','mcq','Sodium valproate is used as a mood stabiliser in bipolar disorder. Which of the following is its most important contraindication in women of childbearing potential?','["A. It causes hyperprolactinaemia reducing fertility","B. It is a potent teratogen associated with neural tube defects, cognitive impairment, and fetal valproate syndrome -- it must not be used in women of childbearing potential unless no alternatives exist and they use reliable contraception","C. It is absolutely contraindicated due to risk of aplastic anaemia in mothers","D. It causes SIADH in pregnancy leading to fetal hyponatraemia"]','B','Valproate is a major human teratogen -- exposed fetuses have a 10% risk of neural tube defects (spina bifida) and approximately 40% risk of neurodevelopmental issues (lower IQ, autism). Due to the Pregnancy Prevention Programme (PPP), valproate must only be prescribed to women of childbearing potential if enrolled in a risk minimisation programme with reliable contraception. This is a patient safety priority.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Mood Stabilisers','hard','mcq','Lamotrigine is used as a mood stabiliser primarily for which phase of bipolar disorder, and what is its most serious rare adverse effect?','["A. Bipolar depression and prevention of depressive episodes; most serious effect is Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS)/toxic epidermal necrolysis, especially with rapid dose escalation or concomitant valproate","B. Rapid cycling bipolar; most serious effect is nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","C. Acute mania; most serious effect is QT prolongation","D. Manic episodes; most serious effect is agranulocytosis"]','C','Lamotrigine is effective for bipolar depression and the prevention of depressive episodes (less effective for mania). Its most serious adverse effect is severe cutaneous reactions including Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN), which are more likely with rapid dose titration or co-administration of valproate (which doubles lamotrigine levels). The dose must be started very low and increased slowly over weeks.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Mood Stabilisers','medium','mcq','A patient presents with acute lithium toxicity (serum level 2.8 mmol/L) with coarse tremor, confusion, and oliguria. What is the most appropriate treatment?','["A. Stop lithium, give aggressive IV saline to restore renal perfusion and increase lithium clearance; consider haemodialysis for severe toxicity","B. Administer flumazenil to reverse sedation while maintaining lithium","C. Activated charcoal and gastric lavage alone","D. Administer IV sodium bicarbonate to alkalinise urine and increase lithium excretion"]','A','Lithium toxicity management: stop lithium immediately, ensure adequate hydration with IV normal saline (volume expansion increases renal lithium excretion). Haemodialysis is indicated for severe toxicity (level >2.5 mmol/L with symptoms, or >4.0 mmol/L regardless of symptoms), oliguria/renal failure, or cardiovascular instability. Activated charcoal is ineffective for lithium. There is no specific antidote.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Mood Stabilisers','hard','mcq','Which statement correctly describes the pharmacokinetic handling of lithium by the kidney?','["A. Lithium is actively secreted by the proximal tubule; inhibition of secretion causes toxicity","B. Lithium is excreted unchanged by the distal tubule independently of sodium balance","C. Lithium is entirely protein-bound and filtered at the glomerulus without reabsorption","D. Lithium is freely filtered at the glomerulus and reabsorbed in the proximal tubule alongside sodium; sodium depletion increases lithium reabsorption and toxicity risk"]','D','Lithium is a monovalent cation handled similarly to sodium by the kidney. It is freely filtered at the glomerulus, and approximately 80% is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule alongside sodium. When sodium is depleted (e.g., due to diuretics, low sodium diet, diarrhoea, or vomiting), the kidney compensatorily reabsorbs more sodium (and lithium), increasing serum lithium levels and risking toxicity. This explains multiple clinically important drug and dietary interactions.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Mood Stabilisers','medium','mcq','Which mood stabiliser is preferred for patients with bipolar disorder who also have comorbid epilepsy, and what monitoring is required?','["A. Lithium -- monitor serum levels and renal function only","B. Sodium valproate -- monitor LFTs (hepatotoxicity risk), FBC (thrombocytopenia), and serum valproate levels","C. Quetiapine -- monitor fasting glucose and lipids","D. Lamotrigine -- monitor FBC and bone marrow function monthly"]','B','Sodium valproate serves dual purpose in bipolar disorder with comorbid epilepsy, providing both mood stabilisation and seizure control. Monitoring requirements include: LFTs (valproate causes hepatotoxicity, rarely fatal, especially in children under 2 years), FBC (thrombocytopenia and platelet dysfunction), serum valproate levels (therapeutic range 50-100 mg/L), ammonia (hyperammonaemia), and weight.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Parkinson''s Disease Drugs','easy','mcq','Why must levodopa be combined with carbidopa when treating Parkinson''s disease?','["A. Carbidopa inhibits peripheral DOPA decarboxylase, reducing conversion of levodopa to dopamine outside the CNS, thereby increasing levodopa availability to the brain and reducing peripheral side effects","B. Carbidopa prevents levodopa from crossing the blood-brain barrier too rapidly","C. Carbidopa inhibits central DOPA decarboxylase to prevent overproduction of dopamine in the brain","D. Carbidopa enhances dopamine receptor sensitivity in the striatum"]','C','Levodopa is converted to dopamine by DOPA decarboxylase. Without carbidopa, over 95% of levodopa is decarboxylated peripherally, producing dopamine in the bloodstream (causing nausea, vomiting, hypotension, arrhythmias) without crossing the blood-brain barrier. Carbidopa does not cross the BBB, so it inhibits only peripheral decarboxylase, allowing more levodopa to reach the brain and reducing the required levodopa dose by 4-5 fold.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Parkinson''s Disease Drugs','medium','mcq','A patient with Parkinson''s disease on levodopa-carbidopa for 4 years begins to experience sudden, unpredictable switching between normal mobility and immobility. What is this phenomenon called?','["A. Tardive dyskinesia from dopamine receptor supersensitivity","B. End-of-dose wearing off due to short levodopa half-life","C. Drug tolerance","D. On-off phenomenon -- unpredictable motor fluctuations thought to result from declining dopaminergic neuronal reserve and pulsatile dopamine receptor stimulation"]','D','The on-off phenomenon consists of sudden, unpredictable switches between mobile (on) and rigid/immobile (off) states, not correlated with levodopa dosing times. It occurs in later stages when dopaminergic neuronal reserve is depleted, and pulsatile receptor stimulation from intermittent levodopa doses leads to receptor desensitisation. Management includes more frequent smaller doses, controlled-release formulations, or addition of dopamine agonists.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Parkinson''s Disease Drugs','medium','mcq','Which MAO-B inhibitor is used as adjunctive therapy in Parkinson''s disease, and what is its rationale?','["A. Tranylcypromine -- non-selectively inhibits MAO, increasing dopamine in the basal ganglia","B. Phenelzine -- inhibits MAO-A and MAO-B equally, preventing dopamine breakdown peripherally","C. Selegiline or rasagiline -- selectively inhibit MAO-B in the brain, reducing dopamine breakdown in the striatum and extending the duration of levodopa effect","D. Moclobemide -- reversibly inhibits MAO-A to prevent dopamine catabolism"]','A','Selegiline and rasagiline selectively inhibit monoamine oxidase type B (MAO-B), the predominant form responsible for dopamine catabolism in the striatum. By reducing dopamine breakdown, they prolong and enhance the effects of both endogenous dopamine and administered levodopa. They are used as adjunctive therapy or (rasagiline) as monotherapy in early Parkinson''s disease. At standard doses, the cheese effect is avoided because MAO-A remains active.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Parkinson''s Disease Drugs','hard','mcq','Amantadine was originally developed as an antiviral drug but is used in Parkinson''s disease. What is its proposed mechanism in this context?','["A. It directly activates dopamine D2 receptors in the striatum","B. It blocks NMDA glutamate receptors and also enhances dopamine release and blocks dopamine reuptake, reducing dyskinesias and improving motor symptoms","C. It inhibits acetylcholinesterase, increasing ACh in the striatum to balance dopamine","D. It blocks muscarinic receptors, reducing cholinergic overactivity in the basal ganglia"]','B','Amantadine''s anti-parkinsonian mechanisms include: NMDA receptor blockade (reducing glutamate excitotoxicity and dyskinesias), enhancement of dopamine release from presynaptic terminals, and blockade of dopamine reuptake. It is most valued in clinical practice for reducing levodopa-induced dyskinesias and can be used as early monotherapy. Its antiviral mechanism (M2 ion channel blockade in influenza) is entirely separate.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Parkinson''s Disease Drugs','easy','mcq','Which of the following is a non-ergot dopamine agonist used in Parkinson''s disease?','["A. Bromocriptine","B. Cabergoline","C. Pramipexole","D. Pergolide"]','C','Non-ergot dopamine agonists include pramipexole, ropinirole, and rotigotine (transdermal patch). They directly stimulate dopamine receptors (D2/D3) in the striatum, independent of neuronal conversion. Ergot-derived agonists (bromocriptine, cabergoline, pergolide) are now rarely used due to fibrotic complications (pulmonary, cardiac, retroperitoneal fibrosis). Non-ergot agonists are preferred, especially in younger Parkinson''s patients.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Parkinson''s Disease Drugs','medium','mcq','A Parkinson''s patient on pramipexole develops episodes of falling asleep suddenly while driving and compulsive gambling. Which drug class effect explains these adverse effects?','["A. MAO-B inhibition causing elevated dopamine leading to hypomanic behaviour","B. Dopamine agonist-induced impulse control disorders and sudden-onset sleep attacks","C. Anticholinergic effect causing sedation and disinhibition","D. NMDA blockade causing dissociative states and risk-taking behaviour"]','D','Dopamine agonists can cause sudden-onset sleep attacks (without warning drowsiness), particularly in early Parkinson''s. They also cause impulse control disorders (gambling, hypersexuality, binge eating, compulsive shopping) in up to 17% of patients, attributed to D3 receptor stimulation in the mesolimbic reward pathway. Patients must be warned about driving risk and counselled on impulse control.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Parkinson''s Disease Drugs','hard','mcq','COMT inhibitors (entacapone, tolcapone) are used as adjuncts to levodopa-carbidopa. What is their mechanism and how does entacapone differ from tolcapone?','["A. Both inhibit catechol-O-methyltransferase peripherally; tolcapone also acts centrally and requires LFT monitoring due to rare fatal hepatotoxicity","B. Both inhibit MAO-B; entacapone is selective for the liver, tolcapone for the brain","C. Both block dopamine reuptake; they differ only in half-life","D. Entacapone inhibits COMT centrally; tolcapone inhibits it peripherally only"]','A','COMT is responsible for peripheral (and some central) methylation of levodopa and dopamine. Entacapone inhibits peripheral COMT only, extending levodopa''s half-life and smoothing motor fluctuations. Tolcapone inhibits both peripheral and central COMT, potentially providing greater benefit, but requires regular LFT monitoring due to rare but potentially fatal hepatotoxicity (fatal cases reported). Entacapone has no hepatotoxicity risk.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Parkinson''s Disease Drugs','medium','mcq','Why should antipsychotic drugs generally be avoided in patients with Parkinson''s disease?','["A. They increase levodopa metabolism, reducing its efficacy","B. They block dopamine D2 receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway, worsening motor symptoms and causing drug-induced parkinsonism","C. They cause irreversible cerebellar degeneration when combined with levodopa","D. They reduce GABA levels in the basal ganglia, worsening tremor"]','B','Parkinson''s disease involves a deficiency of dopamine in the nigrostriatal pathway. Antipsychotics -- particularly typical antipsychotics and many atypicals -- block D2 receptors in this pathway, further reducing dopaminergic signalling and dramatically worsening motor symptoms. If an antipsychotic is needed (e.g., levodopa-induced psychosis), only quetiapine or clozapine (lowest D2 affinity) should be considered.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Parkinson''s Disease Drugs','hard','mcq','A 68-year-old patient with newly diagnosed Parkinson''s disease needs initial therapy. What is the most important factor favouring levodopa over a dopamine agonist in this age group?','["A. Dopamine agonists cause more nausea and vomiting in elderly patients making them unsuitable","B. Levodopa is preferred only because it crosses the blood-brain barrier more easily in older patients","C. Levodopa has fewer long-term complications in elderly patients and causes less risk of hallucinations and impulse control disorders than dopamine agonists","D. Levodopa is cheaper and therefore always preferred regardless of age"]','C','In older patients (over 70 years), levodopa is generally preferred as initial therapy because: dopamine agonists carry a higher risk of hallucinations, confusion, and impulse control disorders in the elderly; levodopa provides more reliable symptomatic control; and older patients are less likely to develop motor complications over their remaining disease course. In younger patients (under 60), dopamine agonists are often preferred to delay levodopa motor complications.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Parkinson''s Disease Drugs','medium','mcq','Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) should be avoided in patients taking which Parkinson''s disease drug formulation, and why?','["A. Entacapone -- pyridoxine induces COMT enzyme activity","B. Levodopa alone (without carbidopa) -- pyridoxine enhances peripheral DOPA decarboxylase activity, increasing conversion of levodopa to dopamine in the periphery and reducing CNS availability","C. Carbidopa-levodopa -- pyridoxine inhibits carbidopa''s action","D. Pramipexole -- pyridoxine competes for D3 receptor binding"]','D','Pyridoxine is a cofactor for DOPA decarboxylase. In patients taking levodopa alone (without carbidopa), supplemental pyridoxine enhances peripheral decarboxylation of levodopa to dopamine, reducing the amount reaching the CNS and diminishing therapeutic effect. This interaction is negated when levodopa is combined with carbidopa, as carbidopa inhibits peripheral decarboxylase regardless of pyridoxine.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Alzheimer''s Disease Drugs','easy','mcq','What is the primary mechanism of action of donepezil in Alzheimer''s disease?','["A. It inhibits beta-secretase, reducing amyloid precursor protein cleavage and amyloid plaque formation","B. It blocks NMDA glutamate receptors, reducing excitotoxic neuronal death","C. It reversibly inhibits acetylcholinesterase, increasing synaptic acetylcholine levels in the cortex and hippocampus","D. It is a muscarinic M1 receptor agonist, directly stimulating cholinergic signalling"]','A','Donepezil is a reversible, selective acetylcholinesterase inhibitor. In Alzheimer''s disease, there is loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert, reducing cortical acetylcholine. By inhibiting the enzyme responsible for ACh breakdown, donepezil increases synaptic ACh concentration, partially compensating for this deficit and improving cognition temporarily. It does not slow disease progression.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Alzheimer''s Disease Drugs','medium','mcq','How does rivastigmine differ from donepezil in its mechanism of action?','["A. Rivastigmine is an irreversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitor with higher selectivity for the cerebral cortex","B. Rivastigmine inhibits both acetylcholinesterase and butyrylcholinesterase (pseudo-cholinesterase) and is available as a transdermal patch","C. Rivastigmine blocks NMDA receptors in addition to inhibiting acetylcholinesterase","D. Rivastigmine is a selective nicotinic acetylcholine receptor agonist unlike donepezil"]','B','Rivastigmine inhibits both acetylcholinesterase (AChE) and butyrylcholinesterase (BuChE), the latter of which is upregulated in Alzheimer''s brain tissue. It forms a pseudo-irreversible carbamyl complex with the enzyme (slow to dissociate). The transdermal patch formulation provides smoother plasma levels and reduces gastrointestinal side effects compared to oral dosing. It is also approved for dementia in Parkinson''s disease.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Alzheimer''s Disease Drugs','medium','mcq','A patient with moderate Alzheimer''s disease is started on memantine. What is its mechanism of action and in which stage is it approved?','["A. Memantine is an uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist that blocks pathological glutamate excitotoxicity; approved for moderate-to-severe Alzheimer''s disease","B. Memantine is a dopamine agonist that improves memory consolidation in early Alzheimer''s disease","C. Memantine inhibits beta-secretase to reduce amyloid plaque formation in all stages","D. Memantine is a cholinesterase inhibitor approved for mild-to-moderate Alzheimer''s disease"]','C','Memantine is a low-affinity, uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist. In Alzheimer''s, chronic low-level glutamate activation of NMDA receptors contributes to excitotoxic neuronal death. Memantine blocks tonically activated NMDA channels without impairing physiological (phasic) synaptic transmission needed for learning. It is approved for moderate-to-severe Alzheimer''s, often in combination with a cholinesterase inhibitor.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Alzheimer''s Disease Drugs','hard','mcq','A 76-year-old with Alzheimer''s disease on donepezil undergoes elective surgery. Which intraoperative interaction should the anaesthetist be aware of?','["A. Donepezil blocks nicotinic receptors, causing resistance to neuromuscular blocking agents","B. Donepezil causes hyperkalaemia, contraindicating thiopentone use","C. Donepezil induces hepatic enzymes, reducing efficacy of anaesthetic agents","D. Donepezil inhibits plasma cholinesterase (pseudocholinesterase), prolonging the action of suxamethonium (succinylcholine) and certain ester-type local anaesthetics"]','D','Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors, including donepezil, can inhibit plasma cholinesterase (butyrylcholinesterase). Suxamethonium is hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase; its action can be significantly prolonged in patients on cholinesterase inhibitors, resulting in a prolonged neuromuscular block. Anaesthetists must be alerted to this interaction pre-operatively.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Alzheimer''s Disease Drugs','easy','mcq','What are the most common adverse effects of cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil?','["A. Hepatotoxicity and bone marrow suppression","B. Extrapyramidal symptoms and tardive dyskinesia","C. Nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, bradycardia, and insomnia -- all due to excess cholinergic stimulation","D. Hypertension, weight gain, and metabolic syndrome"]','A','Cholinesterase inhibitors increase systemic acetylcholine levels, producing cholinergic side effects via muscarinic receptor stimulation: gastrointestinal effects (nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, anorexia), cardiovascular effects (bradycardia, heart block -- relevant in patients with cardiac conduction disease), increased sweating, and sleep disturbances (donepezil can cause vivid dreams or insomnia when taken at night). Bradycardia is the most clinically dangerous.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Alzheimer''s Disease Drugs','medium','mcq','Which statement is most accurate regarding the clinical expectations of cholinesterase inhibitor therapy in Alzheimer''s disease?','["A. They are effective only in early-onset familial Alzheimer''s disease","B. They cure Alzheimer''s disease by eliminating amyloid plaques","C. They slow cognitive decline by approximately 50% compared to placebo","D. They provide modest symptomatic improvement and slow cognitive decline temporarily but do not halt disease progression or modify the underlying neurodegeneration"]','B','Cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine) and memantine provide modest, statistically significant improvements in cognition, global function, and activities of daily living. However, they do not modify the underlying pathology (amyloid plaques, tau tangles), and disease progression continues despite treatment. Realistic counselling of patients and caregivers about the limited but real symptomatic benefits is important.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Alzheimer''s Disease Drugs','hard','mcq','Galantamine has a dual mechanism of action. Beyond acetylcholinesterase inhibition, what additional mechanism contributes to its efficacy in Alzheimer''s disease?','["A. It is an allosteric potentiating ligand (APL) at nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, enhancing ACh-induced ion channel opening","B. It inhibits beta-secretase (BACE-1), reducing amyloid precursor protein cleavage","C. It blocks serotonin 5-HT6 receptors, improving memory consolidation","D. It is an NMDA receptor antagonist, reducing glutamate excitotoxicity"]','C','Galantamine has a unique dual mechanism: it reversibly inhibits AChE (increasing synaptic ACh) AND acts as an allosteric potentiating ligand (APL) at nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, enhancing receptor sensitivity to ACh. The nicotinic stimulation may additionally enhance release of other neurotransmitters (serotonin, GABA, glutamate) that modulate cognition. This dual action theoretically provides broader synaptic enhancement than AChE inhibition alone.','universal',false,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Alzheimer''s Disease Drugs','medium','mcq','In which situation would it be appropriate to use both a cholinesterase inhibitor AND memantine in the same Alzheimer''s patient?','["A. Only when the patient refuses oral donepezil and needs a different mechanism","B. Only in patients with concurrent Parkinson''s disease dementia","C. Mild Alzheimer''s disease when the patient is not responding to memantine alone","D. Moderate-to-severe Alzheimer''s disease where combination therapy provides additional cognitive and functional benefit over either agent alone"]','D','In moderate-to-severe Alzheimer''s disease, combining a cholinesterase inhibitor with memantine is evidence-based and guideline-recommended. The two agents have complementary mechanisms (cholinergic enhancement vs NMDA blockade) and the combination has been shown to provide additional benefit in cognition, behaviour, and activities of daily living compared to cholinesterase inhibitor monotherapy at this stage.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Alzheimer''s Disease Drugs','hard','mcq','A recently approved monoclonal antibody for early Alzheimer''s disease targets and removes amyloid-beta plaques. Which of the following describes the class of drug represented by lecanemab or aducanumab?','["A. Gamma-secretase inhibitors that divert APP processing away from the amyloidogenic pathway","B. BACE-1 inhibitors that prevent amyloid precursor protein cleavage","C. Anti-amyloid monoclonal antibodies that clear amyloid-beta aggregates via immune-mediated mechanisms, representing the first disease-modifying therapies for early Alzheimer''s","D. Tau aggregation inhibitors that prevent neurofibrillary tangle formation"]','A','Lecanemab (Leqembi) and aducanumab represent a new class of anti-amyloid monoclonal antibodies that bind to and facilitate immune clearance of amyloid-beta aggregates and plaques. They are the first agents to demonstrate disease modification (slowing of clinical decline) in early/mild Alzheimer''s disease. Key adverse effects include amyloid-related imaging abnormalities (ARIA) -- cerebral oedema and microhaemorrhages visible on MRI.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Alzheimer''s Disease Drugs','medium','mcq','Which neurotransmitter deficiency in the nucleus basalis of Meynert is most directly targeted by current pharmacotherapy for Alzheimer''s disease?','["A. Dopamine -- replaced by levodopa","B. Serotonin -- enhanced by SSRI antidepressants","C. Acetylcholine -- enhanced by cholinesterase inhibitors","D. Noradrenaline -- enhanced by SNRIs"]','C','The cholinergic hypothesis of Alzheimer''s disease posits that degeneration of cholinergic neurons projecting from the nucleus basalis of Meynert (basal forebrain) to the cortex and hippocampus underlies the cognitive deficits. This deficit in acetylcholine is the pharmacological target of all cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine) currently approved for symptomatic treatment of Alzheimer''s disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Opioid Analgesics','easy','mcq','Which receptor is primarily responsible for the analgesic, euphoric, and respiratory depressant effects of morphine?','["A. Sigma receptor","B. Delta opioid receptor","C. Kappa opioid receptor","D. Mu opioid receptor"]','B','Morphine and most clinically used opioids are primarily mu opioid receptor agonists. Mu receptor activation mediates: supraspinal and spinal analgesia, euphoria and reward (mesolimbic), respiratory depression (brainstem), decreased GI motility (constipation), and physical dependence. Delta receptors also contribute to analgesia; kappa receptors mediate spinal analgesia, sedation, and dysphoria.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Opioid Analgesics','medium','mcq','A patient with severe opioid overdose presents with pinpoint pupils, respiratory rate 4/min, and unconsciousness. What is the most appropriate immediate pharmacological intervention?','["A. Activated charcoal via nasogastric tube","B. Dantrolene IV for muscle rigidity","C. Flumazenil IV to reverse sedation","D. Naloxone IV -- a competitive opioid antagonist that rapidly reverses respiratory depression, sedation, and miosis"]','D','Naloxone is a competitive antagonist at mu, delta, and kappa opioid receptors. Given IV, it rapidly reverses opioid-induced respiratory depression, sedation, and miosis within 1-2 minutes. Its short half-life (60-90 minutes) means repeated doses or an infusion may be needed for longer-acting opioids like methadone. It also precipitates acute withdrawal in opioid-dependent patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Opioid Analgesics','medium','mcq','Why is codeine considered a prodrug and why is its use restricted in certain patient populations?','["A. Codeine is converted to morphine by CYP2D6; ultra-rapid metabolisers produce toxic morphine levels; poor metabolisers get no analgesia -- restricted in children and breastfeeding mothers","B. Codeine requires conversion by CYP3A4; contraindicated in renal failure due to accumulation of toxic metabolites","C. Codeine is activated by glucuronidation to codeine-6-glucuronide; this accumulates in elderly patients","D. Codeine is a prodrug requiring CYP2C19 activation; poor metabolisers are at risk of accumulation"]','A','Codeine is O-demethylated to morphine by CYP2D6. Ultra-rapid metabolisers (with CYP2D6 gene duplications) convert codeine to morphine very rapidly, producing dangerously high morphine levels. Following reports of infant deaths when breastfeeding mothers were ultra-rapid metabolisers, codeine is now contraindicated in children under 12, nursing mothers, and post-tonsillectomy/adenoidectomy patients.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Opioid Analgesics','hard','mcq','What is the mechanism of opioid-induced constipation and why does tolerance NOT develop to this side effect?','["A. Opioids block enteric serotonin receptors; tolerance develops because receptor expression increases","B. Opioids activate mu receptors in the enteric nervous system, reducing propulsive peristalsis, increasing sphincter tone, and reducing secretions; tolerance does not develop because peripheral opioid receptors do not downregulate as readily as central receptors","C. Opioids inhibit substance P in the spinal cord, eliminating visceral pain signalling -- effect wanes with tolerance","D. Constipation is due to dehydration from ADH-like effects -- tolerance develops as kidney adapts"]','B','Mu opioid receptors in the myenteric (Auerbach''s) and submucosal (Meissner''s) plexuses mediate opioid-induced constipation (OIC). Activation inhibits acetylcholine release from enteric neurons, reducing propulsive contractions, increasing pyloric and ileocaecal sphincter tone, reducing intestinal secretions, and increasing water absorption. Crucially, tolerance does not develop to OIC at the same rate as to analgesia, meaning long-term opioid users require laxatives indefinitely. Peripherally acting mu-opioid antagonists (naloxegol, methylnaltrexone) treat OIC without reversing central analgesia.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Opioid Analgesics','easy','mcq','Which opioid is most appropriate for managing acute severe pain in a patient with renal failure, and why?','["A. Fentanyl -- preferred in renal failure because it has no active metabolites that accumulate; metabolised by CYP3A4 to inactive products","B. Codeine -- no dose adjustment required in renal failure","C. Tramadol -- renally cleared without active metabolite accumulation","D. Morphine -- safest in renal failure due to its short half-life"]','C','Morphine is metabolised to morphine-6-glucuronide (M6G), an active and potent metabolite that accumulates in renal failure, causing prolonged sedation and respiratory depression. Fentanyl is extensively metabolised by CYP3A4 to inactive metabolites (norfentanyl), with no clinically relevant active metabolite accumulation in renal failure. It is therefore the preferred strong opioid in this setting. Tramadol and codeine also have active metabolites that accumulate.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Opioid Analgesics','medium','mcq','Tramadol has a dual mechanism of action. Which two mechanisms contribute to its analgesic effect?','["A. Sigma receptor agonism and alpha-2 adrenoceptor stimulation","B. Mu opioid receptor agonism and blockade of serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake","C. Kappa receptor agonism and NMDA receptor blockade","D. Mu opioid receptor agonism and cyclooxygenase inhibition"]','D','Tramadol provides analgesia through: (1) weak mu opioid receptor agonism (direct and via its active metabolite O-desmethyltramadol, produced by CYP2D6), and (2) inhibition of serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake, activating descending pain inhibitory pathways. This dual mechanism contributes to analgesic efficacy in neuropathic pain. It also increases seizure risk and can precipitate serotonin syndrome with SSRIs/MAOIs.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Opioid Analgesics','hard','mcq','Which phenomenon explains why opioid-dependent patients require escalating doses to achieve the same degree of analgesia over time?','["A. Metabolic tolerance -- increased plasma protein binding reduces free drug fraction","B. Pharmacokinetic tolerance -- opioids induce CYP3A4, increasing their own metabolism","C. Pharmacodynamic tolerance -- involving mu receptor desensitisation (uncoupling from G-proteins), internalisation, and downregulation with chronic activation","D. Receptor upregulation -- mu receptors increase in number, requiring more drug to occupy them"]','A','Opioid tolerance is primarily pharmacodynamic. Chronic mu receptor activation leads to: receptor desensitisation (phosphorylation uncouples receptor from Gi protein via GRK/beta-arrestin pathways), receptor internalisation (endocytosis reduces surface receptor density), and downstream signal transduction adaptations. This means progressively higher doses are needed for the same analgesic effect, contributing to the escalation cycle in chronic opioid use.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Opioid Analgesics','medium','mcq','A heroin-dependent patient enters withdrawal 12 hours after his last dose. Which clinical features are characteristic of opioid withdrawal?','["A. Pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression, hypotension, and hypothermia","B. Mydriasis, lacrimation, rhinorrhoea, piloerection (goosebumps), muscle cramps, diarrhoea, tachycardia, and hypertension","C. Seizures, hyperthermia, severe autonomic instability, and rhabdomyolysis","D. Euphoria, sedation, and constipation"]','B','Opioid withdrawal is the physiological opposite of opioid intoxication. Cessation causes a sympathetic rebound: mydriasis (not miosis), lacrimation, rhinorrhoea, yawning, diaphoresis, piloerection (cold turkey), severe muscle cramps, diarrhoea, vomiting, tachycardia, hypertension, and intense drug craving. Although extremely distressing, opioid withdrawal is rarely life-threatening in otherwise healthy adults (unlike alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Opioid Analgesics','easy','mcq','Methadone is used for opioid substitution therapy. Which pharmacological properties make it particularly suitable for this indication?','["A. Long half-life (24-36+ hours) providing stable blood levels without pronounced peaks/troughs, reducing cravings and withdrawal; once-daily oral dosing enables supervised consumption","B. It is a partial mu agonist with a ceiling effect on respiratory depression, making it extremely safe","C. It has no cross-tolerance with heroin, making it effective in treatment-resistant opioid dependence","D. Short half-life with rapid peaks allowing supervised daily dosing without withdrawal"]','C','Methadone''s suitability for opioid substitution therapy rests on: its long half-life (24-36 hours, sometimes up to 60 hours) providing stable plasma levels and preventing withdrawal; oral bioavailability enabling supervised once-daily dispensing; and its ability to reduce illicit drug use, crime, and HIV transmission risk. Key risks include QT prolongation, drug interactions via CYP3A4/CYP2D6, and accumulation risk in hepatic impairment.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Opioid Analgesics','medium','mcq','Which opioid is a partial mu agonist and is combined with naloxone in a sublingual formulation to deter intravenous misuse?','["A. Codeine with naloxone","B. Methadone with naloxone","C. Tramadol with naloxone","D. Buprenorphine with naloxone (Suboxone)"]','D','Buprenorphine is a partial mu agonist (and kappa antagonist) with very high receptor affinity. Its partial agonism produces a ceiling effect on respiratory depression, making it safer in overdose. Combined with naloxone in a sublingual formulation (Suboxone), when taken as directed, the naloxone has minimal absorption and effect. If injected IV, naloxone is rapidly absorbed and precipitates withdrawal, deterring misuse. Used for opioid dependence treatment and increasingly for chronic pain.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics','easy','mcq','What is the primary mechanism of action of local anaesthetics such as lidocaine?','["A. They competitively block nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at sensory nerve terminals","B. They open voltage-gated potassium channels, hyperpolarising the nerve membrane","C. They block voltage-gated sodium channels from the intracellular side in a use-dependent manner, preventing action potential propagation","D. They activate inhibitory GABA-A receptors, hyperpolarising pain fibres"]','A','Local anaesthetics (LAs) diffuse across the nerve membrane and bind to voltage-gated sodium channels from the intracellular side, preventing sodium influx and blocking action potential generation and propagation. Their binding is use-dependent (state-dependent) -- they preferentially bind to open or inactivated channels (during repeated firing), explaining why actively firing pain fibres are blocked more readily than resting fibres.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics','medium','mcq','Which of the following correctly distinguishes ester-type from amide-type local anaesthetics?','["A. Esters are metabolised by the liver; amides are hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase","B. Amides are metabolised by hepatic enzymes (CYP); esters are hydrolysed by plasma pseudocholinesterase producing PABA, which can cause allergic reactions","C. Both classes are metabolised identically by the liver with equal allergy risk","D. Esters have a longer duration of action than amides due to slower hydrolysis"]','B','Ester local anaesthetics (cocaine, procaine, benzocaine, tetracaine) are hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase (pseudocholinesterase). One metabolite is para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA), which can cause type I hypersensitivity/allergic reactions. Amide local anaesthetics (lidocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine, prilocaine) are metabolised by hepatic CYP enzymes (primarily CYP3A4/1A2) and are associated with very rare true allergic reactions.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics','medium','mcq','Why is adrenaline (epinephrine) added to local anaesthetic solutions for infiltration anaesthesia?','["A. It causes local vasoconstriction, reducing systemic absorption, prolonging duration of action, and reducing bleeding in the surgical field","B. It prevents local anaesthetic precipitation in tissue fluids","C. It counteracts the systemic vasodilation caused by local anaesthetic absorption","D. It enhances the blockade of sodium channels synergistically with the local anaesthetic"]','C','Adding adrenaline (typically 1:200,000) to local anaesthetic solutions causes vasoconstriction at the injection site, reducing local blood flow. This: (1) slows systemic absorption, lowering peak plasma concentration and toxicity risk; (2) prolongs the duration of local anaesthesia by keeping the drug at its site of action; and (3) reduces bleeding. It must NOT be used in ring blocks (digits, penis, nose, ear) due to risk of ischaemic necrosis from vasoconstriction in end-arteries.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics','hard','mcq','A patient receiving bupivacaine epidural analgesia during labour inadvertently receives an intravascular injection and develops cardiovascular collapse. Why is bupivacaine particularly cardiotoxic compared to lidocaine?','["A. Bupivacaine inhibits cardiac calcium channels more potently than lidocaine","B. Bupivacaine crosses the placenta more readily, causing foetal cardiac toxicity that manifests as maternal cardiovascular collapse","C. Bupivacaine is more water-soluble, allowing faster renal clearance but higher initial cardiac toxicity","D. Bupivacaine has high lipophilicity and very slow dissociation from cardiac sodium channels (fast in, slow out), causing prolonged sodium channel blockade in cardiac muscle and refractory ventricular arrhythmias"]','D','Bupivacaine dissociates very slowly from cardiac sodium channels. This fast in, slow out kinetics means that during the diastolic interval, there is insufficient time for complete unbinding, leading to cumulative sodium channel blockade in the heart. This results in bradycardia, heart block, and refractory ventricular arrhythmias. Treatment includes IV lipid emulsion (Intralipid) which sequesters bupivacaine. Levobupivacaine and ropivacaine are less cardiotoxic alternatives.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics','easy','mcq','Which of the following local anaesthetics can be used topically on mucous membranes and is also a drug of abuse?','["A. Cocaine","B. Prilocaine","C. Lidocaine","D. Bupivacaine"]','A','Cocaine is unique among local anaesthetics: it is an ester-type LA that additionally blocks the reuptake of noradrenaline, dopamine, and serotonin, causing CNS stimulation, euphoria, and intense vasoconstriction. This vasoconstriction makes it useful in nasal/ENT procedures (reduces bleeding). Chronic use leads to septal necrosis. It is a Schedule II drug due to its high abuse potential.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics','medium','mcq','Prilocaine in large doses can cause methaemoglobinaemia. Which metabolite is responsible and how is this treated?','["A. Prilocaine is metabolised to PABA, which oxidises haemoglobin, treated with activated charcoal","B. Prilocaine causes haemolysis through complement activation, treated with fresh frozen plasma","C. Prilocaine is converted to hydrogen peroxide in erythrocytes, oxidising haemoglobin, treated with ascorbic acid only","D. Prilocaine is metabolised to ortho-toluidine, which oxidises haemoglobin iron from Fe2+ to Fe3+ (methaemoglobin), treated with methylene blue IV"]','B','Prilocaine is metabolised in the liver to ortho-toluidine, which oxidises the ferrous (Fe2+) iron in haemoglobin to ferric (Fe3+) iron, forming methaemoglobin. Methaemoglobin cannot carry oxygen, causing cyanosis and functional anaemia. Treatment is IV methylene blue 1-2 mg/kg, which reduces methaemoglobin back to haemoglobin. This is clinically relevant in topical anaesthetic creams (EMLA contains prilocaine).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics','medium','mcq','Local anaesthetics are less effective in infected tissue. Which pharmacological principle explains this?','["A. Infected tissue has lower pH due to lactic acid production; local anaesthetics are weak bases, and acidic pH shifts equilibrium toward the ionised form which cannot cross membranes -- reducing intracellular access to sodium channels","B. Inflammation increases local blood flow, washing out the local anaesthetic faster than it can act","C. Bacterial toxins competitively block local anaesthetic binding to sodium channels","D. Bacterial enzymes rapidly metabolise local anaesthetic molecules at the site of infection"]','C','Local anaesthetics are weak bases that exist in equilibrium between ionised (charged, membrane-impermeable) and un-ionised (uncharged, lipophilic, membrane-permeable) forms. At physiological pH, enough un-ionised form crosses the membrane; the ionised form then binds the sodium channel intracellularly. In infected tissue, the pH drops (acidic environment), shifting equilibrium toward the ionised form. Less un-ionised drug crosses the membrane, reducing efficacy.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics','hard','mcq','What is the order of nerve fibre sensitivity to local anaesthetic blockade from most to least sensitive?','["A. A-alpha (motor) > A-beta > A-delta > C fibres","B. All fibre types are equally sensitive; only the dose determines which fibres are blocked","C. A-alpha > A-beta > A-delta > C fibres","D. C fibres (unmyelinated, slow pain/autonomic) > A-delta (sharp pain, temperature) > A-beta (touch) > A-alpha (motor)"]','D','Local anaesthetics block small-diameter and unmyelinated fibres before large myelinated fibres. The sequence is: C fibres (pain, autonomic -- unmyelinated, smallest) are most sensitive; followed by A-delta (sharp pain, temperature); then A-beta (touch, pressure); and finally A-alpha (proprioception, motor) are most resistant. This explains the differential block seen in epidural analgesia: pain is blocked before motor function, enabling pain-free labour without complete paralysis.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics','easy','mcq','A patient develops features of local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST): perioral tingling, metallic taste, tinnitus, followed by seizures and cardiac arrest. What is the recommended treatment beyond standard resuscitation?','["A. IV atropine alone to treat the bradycardia component of LAST","B. IV dextrose 50% to correct hypoglycaemia causing seizures","C. IV lipid emulsion (Intralipid 20%) which acts as a lipid sink, sequestering the lipophilic local anaesthetic and restoring cardiac function","D. IV naloxone to reverse opioid-like sedation"]','A','Intravenous lipid emulsion (ILE/Intralipid 20%) is the specific treatment for severe LAST. The proposed mechanism is the lipid sink effect -- the lipid emulsion creates a separate lipid compartment in the plasma that sequesters lipophilic local anaesthetic (particularly bupivacaine), reducing its concentration at cardiac sodium channels and restoring cardiac function. Standard ACLS is also required. ILE is now part of all major LAST guidelines.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Local Anaesthetics','medium','mcq','Which local anaesthetic is most commonly used for intravenous regional anaesthesia (Bier''s block) and why?','["A. Ropivacaine -- due to its selectivity for sensory over motor fibres","B. Bupivacaine -- due to its long duration of action","C. Cocaine -- due to its intrinsic vasoconstrictive properties","D. Lidocaine (lignocaine) -- due to its relatively low cardiac toxicity and safety profile if the tourniquet fails or deflates early"]','B','In Bier''s block, the entire limb venous system is filled with local anaesthetic after exsanguination and tourniquet application. If the tourniquet deflates prematurely, a large bolus of LA enters the systemic circulation. Lidocaine is preferred because its relatively rapid cardiac sodium channel dissociation makes it far less cardiotoxic than bupivacaine in this scenario. Bupivacaine is absolutely contraindicated for Bier''s block due to reports of fatal cardiac arrest.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Drugs of Abuse & CNS Stimulants','easy','mcq','What is the primary mechanism by which cocaine produces euphoria and reward?','["A. It blocks the dopamine reuptake transporter (DAT) in the mesolimbic pathway, increasing synaptic dopamine in the nucleus accumbens","B. It stimulates serotonin release in the raphe nuclei, producing euphoria","C. It inhibits dopamine metabolism by blocking MAO-A in the striatum","D. It is a direct dopamine D2 receptor agonist in the nucleus accumbens"]','C','Cocaine blocks the dopamine transporter (DAT), preventing dopamine reuptake in mesolimbic neurons projecting to the nucleus accumbens. This accumulation of dopamine in the reward pathway produces intense euphoria. Cocaine also blocks NET and SERT. Its sympathomimetic effects (tachycardia, hypertension, vasoconstriction, mydriasis) result from noradrenaline accumulation. Cardiovascular complications (MI, stroke, arrhythmias) are the leading cause of cocaine-related death.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Drugs of Abuse & CNS Stimulants','medium','mcq','Amphetamines produce sympathomimetic effects through which mechanism that distinguishes them from cocaine?','["A. Amphetamines directly stimulate postsynaptic adrenoreceptors, bypassing the presynaptic terminal","B. Amphetamines depolarise adrenergic nerve terminals by opening voltage-gated calcium channels","C. Amphetamines block DAT and NET competitively, identical in mechanism to cocaine","D. Amphetamines are substrates for DAT and NET, entering terminals and triggering reverse transport -- actively releasing dopamine and noradrenaline into the synapse -- while also inhibiting MAO and displacing vesicular stores"]','D','Amphetamines enter the presynaptic terminal via DAT and NET (as substrates, not blockers). Inside, they: (1) cause reverse transport -- making DAT/NET run in reverse, actively pumping dopamine/noradrenaline into the synapse; (2) displace catecholamines from synaptic vesicles (via VMAT2 reversal); and (3) inhibit MAO. This produces a massive, non-exocytotic release of catecholamines, exceeding that achievable by simple reuptake blockade.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Drugs of Abuse & CNS Stimulants','medium','mcq','A 22-year-old presents with agitation, diaphoresis, mydriasis, hypertension, and hyperthermia after using MDMA (ecstasy) at a nightclub. Which electrolyte disturbance is a potentially fatal complication?','["A. Hyponatraemia -- MDMA causes SIADH via serotonin-mediated ADH release, compounded by excessive water intake in hot environments, leading to cerebral oedema","B. Hypercalcaemia from rhabdomyolysis-induced calcium release from muscle","C. Hypernatraemia from dehydration and water avoidance","D. Hypokalaemia from excessive diaphoresis and catecholamine excess"]','A','MDMA (3,4-methylenedioxymethamphetamine) releases massive serotonin, dopamine, and noradrenaline. Serotonin stimulates hypothalamic neurons to release ADH (SIADH), causing water retention. Combined with hyperthermia-driven excessive water intake (often encouraged as harm reduction in nightclub settings), this causes dilutional hyponatraemia, which can cause cerebral oedema, seizures, and death -- more common in young women.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Drugs of Abuse & CNS Stimulants','hard','mcq','What is the primary mechanism of action of cannabis (THC) in the CNS and which endogenous system does it modulate?','["A. THC is a full agonist at mu opioid receptors, causing euphoria and analgesia via the same pathway as morphine","B. THC is a partial agonist at CB1 endocannabinoid receptors in the CNS, inhibiting adenylyl cyclase and modulating presynaptic neurotransmitter release via Gi-coupled signalling","C. THC blocks dopamine D3 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway, reducing dopamine release and producing calming effects","D. THC stimulates GABA-A receptors, causing sedation through a mechanism identical to benzodiazepines"]','B','Delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) is the primary psychoactive component of cannabis. It acts as a partial agonist at cannabinoid CB1 receptors (predominantly central, especially in hippocampus, basal ganglia, cerebellum, and prefrontal cortex) and CB2 receptors (peripheral, immune cells). CB1 receptors are Gi-coupled -- activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase (reducing cAMP), inhibits voltage-gated calcium channels, and opens potassium channels, collectively reducing presynaptic neurotransmitter release.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Drugs of Abuse & CNS Stimulants','easy','mcq','What is the mechanism by which chronic alcohol use leads to CNS depressant effects, and what pharmacological principle underlies the risk of seizures on abrupt withdrawal?','["A. Alcohol acutely potentiates GABA-A and inhibits NMDA receptors; chronic use leads to compensatory downregulation of GABA-A and upregulation of NMDA receptors; abrupt cessation causes unrestrained CNS hyperexcitability and seizures","B. Alcohol inhibits sodium channels acutely; withdrawal unmasks excess sodium influx causing seizures","C. Alcohol blocks opioid receptors; withdrawal produces hyperalgesia that triggers seizures","D. Alcohol permanently damages GABA-A receptors; withdrawal seizures occur due to structural receptor loss"]','C','Alcohol acutely enhances GABA-A receptor function (inhibitory) and inhibits NMDA glutamate receptors (excitatory), producing CNS depression. Chronic exposure causes homeostatic adaptation: GABA-A receptors downregulate/desensitise and NMDA receptors upregulate. When alcohol is abruptly stopped, the compensatory changes are unmasked -- reduced inhibition and increased excitation -- causing severe CNS hyperexcitability, manifesting as tremor, seizures (typically 24-48 hours), and delirium tremens (48-72 hours).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Drugs of Abuse & CNS Stimulants','medium','mcq','A patient with alcohol dependence is prescribed disulfiram. What should he be counselled about?','["A. Disulfiram prevents alcohol absorption from the gut, so alcohol can be consumed safely","B. Disulfiram blocks dopamine reward pathways, reducing alcohol craving without any reaction on consumption","C. Disulfiram is safe to take with small amounts of alcohol; the reaction only occurs at high doses","D. Disulfiram inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase; even small amounts of alcohol cause accumulation of acetaldehyde, producing a highly aversive reaction (flushing, palpitations, nausea, vomiting, headache) -- alcohol must be completely avoided including in food, mouthwashes, and medications"]','D','Disulfiram (Antabuse) irreversibly inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH), blocking the metabolism of acetaldehyde (the first metabolite of alcohol). Acetaldehyde accumulation causes the intensely unpleasant disulfiram-alcohol reaction: facial flushing, severe nausea and vomiting, tachycardia, hypotension, and dyspnoea. Even small amounts of alcohol in foods, perfumes, or medications can trigger the reaction. It is used as aversion therapy to support abstinence.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Drugs of Abuse & CNS Stimulants','hard','mcq','Which pharmacological agent approved for alcohol dependence reduces craving through restoration of glutamate/GABA balance, and is distinct from naltrexone and disulfiram?','["A. Topiramate -- which blocks AMPA receptors to reduce alcohol-induced glutamate surges","B. Baclofen -- a GABA-B agonist used off-label to reduce craving","C. Acamprosate -- a structural analogue of GABA that modulates NMDA glutamate receptors and GABA-A receptors, reducing the hyperglutamatergic state associated with alcohol withdrawal and craving","D. Buprenorphine -- a partial opioid agonist that reduces alcohol reward"]','A','Acamprosate (calcium acetylhomotaurinate) is a structural analogue of GABA approved for maintaining abstinence in alcohol-dependent patients after detoxification. It is thought to modulate hyperglutamatergic activity (via NMDA receptor interaction) and restore the GABA/glutamate balance disrupted by chronic alcohol use. Unlike naltrexone (opioid antagonist) or disulfiram (ALDH inhibitor), it acts on the glutamatergic system and is renally excreted -- contraindicated in severe renal failure.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Drugs of Abuse & CNS Stimulants','medium','mcq','A cocaine user presents to the emergency department with chest pain, diaphoresis, and ST-elevation on ECG. Which drug is CONTRAINDICATED in the management of cocaine-induced myocardial infarction and why?','["A. GTN (nitroglycerin) -- it potentiates cocaine vasoconstriction via NO pathway interaction","B. Aspirin -- it increases cocaine toxicity by inhibiting prostaglandin-mediated vasodilation","C. Benzodiazepines -- they worsen cocaine-induced hypertension by blocking GABA","D. Non-selective beta-blockers (e.g., propranolol) -- they block beta-2-mediated vasodilation, leaving alpha-1 vasoconstriction unopposed, potentially worsening coronary vasospasm"]','B','In cocaine-induced coronary vasospasm/MI, non-selective beta-blockers are contraindicated. Cocaine causes sympathomimetic effects via both alpha (vasoconstriction) and beta receptors (vasodilation, chronotropy). Blocking beta-2 receptors with non-selective agents leaves alpha-1 adrenoceptors unopposed, worsening coronary vasospasm (paradoxical hypertension and increased coronary vasoconstriction). Treatment includes benzodiazepines (first-line), GTN, aspirin, and phentolamine (alpha-blocker).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Drugs of Abuse & CNS Stimulants','hard','mcq','Which pharmacological agent is approved for alcohol dependence and works by reducing the reinforcing euphoric effect of alcohol via opioid receptor blockade?','["A. Naltrexone -- a mu opioid receptor antagonist that prevents alcohol from activating the endorphin-dopamine reward pathway, reducing craving and relapse rates","B. Acamprosate -- a GABA analogue that restores glutamate/GABA balance","C. Clonidine -- an alpha-2 agonist that suppresses autonomic symptoms of craving","D. Disulfiram -- inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase to create an aversive reaction"]','C','Naltrexone blocks mu opioid receptors, interrupting the alcohol-endorphin-dopamine reinforcement cascade. Alcohol consumption stimulates endogenous opioid release (endorphins), which in turn drives mesolimbic dopamine release and rewarding effects. By blocking mu receptors, naltrexone reduces the reward value of alcohol and decreases relapse rates. It is available as oral daily dosing or as a monthly extended-release injectable (Vivitrol), improving adherence.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Pharmacology','Drugs of Abuse & CNS Stimulants','medium','mcq','Which clinical feature most reliably distinguishes stimulant intoxication (cocaine/amphetamine) from a manic episode in a patient presenting with agitation and pressured speech?','["A. Elevated mood and decreased need for sleep -- these occur in both conditions and do not distinguish them","B. Pupillary dilation (mydriasis), diaphoresis, elevated BP and HR, and nasal septal perforation (cocaine) are more suggestive of stimulant intoxication; urine toxicology screen is the most reliable test","C. Psychosis is never seen in stimulant intoxication, so its presence confirms mania","D. The presence of grandiose delusions -- seen in mania but never in stimulant intoxication"]','D','Stimulant intoxication and mania can be clinically indistinguishable on mental state examination alone (both cause euphoria, decreased sleep, grandiosity, pressured speech, and risk-taking). Physical signs of sympathetic excess (tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, mydriasis, diaphoresis) and signs of chronic stimulant use (nasal septal perforation with cocaine, skin picking, dental erosion with methamphetamine) point to stimulant intoxication. Urine drug screening is the definitive test.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed');
